BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/MAP-Website-5.jpg
CATEGORIES:Workshops,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20261006T111503Z
DESCRIPTION:How do photographs shape the ways we remember\, understand and narrate history? Inspired by Grip\, Hold\, Pin\, this workshop with Managing Editor of TAKE on Art  Dilpreet Bhullar explores the art of writing about photography.\n\nThrough close readings\, discussions and writing exercises\, participants will consider how photographs construct meaning\, and what they reveal or leave unsaid about memory\, identity and history. Moving between exhibition reviews\, critical essays and personal responses\, the workshop invites participants to slow down\, look closely and develop new ways of writing about images.\n\nParticipants are required to bring a personal photograph or an object of their choice as a starting point for reflection and writing.
DTEND:20261010T103000Z
DTSTART:20261010T053000Z
SUMMARY:Photography in Medias-Res
UID:1413@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/photography-in-medias-res-memoir-and-memory/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/artists_for_artists_round_2__067-scaled.jpg
CATEGORIES:Talks,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20261006T124316Z
DESCRIPTION:In the second edition of Shared Futures\, a talk series in conjunction with the exhibition\, Beneath the Turning Sky\, we take a close look into visual artist Rai’s practice and the different ways her work develops—allowing things to grow and shift along the way\, with play\, chance and frugality playing an important role in the process.\n\nIn conversation with curator Priya Chauhan\, she will also touch on the suggestive interactions that are often present of her installations\, and the ways these can shape an encounter with the work. Search Party\, a video work that forms a pair with the painting that is part of the exhibition will also be screened.\nThe session will be followed by a hands-on workshop exploring invisible inks and ideas around latency.\nBeneath the Turning Sky brings together artists working across painting\, sculpture\, photography\, and textile practices\, illuminating contemporary debates around ecology\, technology\, development\, and our relationship with the environment. Through distinct visual languages\, the exhibition asks: How might art reshape our collective stewardship of the planet?\n\nExtending these questions beyond the gallery\, Shared Futures creates a space for exchange through lectures\, conversations\, workshops\, and other experimental formats. Across these encounters\, the series considers what the arts can offer in turbulent times and how creative practices can help forge new ways of thinking\, being\, and building solidarity.\n\nThis series is supported by Rohini Nilekani Philanthropies Foundation
DTEND:20261011T110000Z
DTSTART:20261011T090000Z
SUMMARY:Invisible Inks
UID:1410@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/invisible-inks/
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR